[{"result":{"data":{"json":{"id":"1569","stub":{"id":"1569","section":"1) Jones Creek Day Use Area to Milepost 15","altname":"The Jones Creek Section","river":"Wilson","difficulty":"III","states":["USA-ORE"]},"primaryGaugeStatus":{"status":"below-recommended","latestReading":{"value":"250","dateTime":"2026-05-12T02:30:00.000Z","qualifier":"NORMAL"},"metric":"cfs","adjustedDifficulty":null},"pointOfInterests":[{"type":"put-in","description":"The photo shows boaters in the water upstream from the bridge on river left, but the best access is downstream from the bridge on river right. Drive into the Day Use Area parking and park in front of the information kiosk. There is an easy trail to the river there.","id":"111908","location":{"latitude":"45.587997","longitude":"-123.556303"},"name":"Jones Creek Day-Use Area Put In","distance":"0","photoID":"890603","difficulty":"N/A","approximate":false},{"type":"access","description":"The turnoff from the highway is hidden below the edge of the highway until you are on top of it. The parking area is well above river level and there is a developed trail down to a large river left beach and eddy.","id":"8KMLwGfDKq3j4Y1F3GbL8","location":{"latitude":"45.563099","longitude":"-123.585299"},"name":"Footbridge Trailhead Access","distance":"2.95","photoID":null,"difficulty":"N/A","approximate":false},{"type":"access","description":"This is a rough fisherman's trail up to a pullout alongside the highway. It is possible to park across the highway at the bottom of the Jordan Creek Road, but then you have to carry boats across the highway which is likely the most dangerous thing you'll do all day.","id":"50gqbmdrUd1hi8erggA1w","location":{"latitude":"45.547012","longitude":"-123.603171"},"name":"Jordan Creek Access","distance":"4.97","photoID":null,"difficulty":"N/A","approximate":false},{"type":"access","description":"This access point is on Cedar Butte Road but some call it the Wolf Creek Road access. Both roads are right there. It's on river right, and requires carrying your boat up a trail from a smallish eddy on the right. At high flows the eddy gets hard to catch because the current diverts boats away from it.","id":"vkhNF6UPjYJ4h8RuNBPnh","location":{"latitude":"45.54776314","longitude":"-123.5937451"},"name":"Cedar Butte Road access","distance":"5.3","photoID":null,"difficulty":"N/A","approximate":false},{"type":"takeout","description":"This is the takeout above the Narrows. It is just a pullout alongside the guard rail. From the river it's a rock landing then a steep scramble up to here. Definitely scout the get-out. To get turned around to drive back upstream, go downstream to the outside of the next curve, then turn left from there.","id":"117181","location":{"latitude":"45.510115","longitude":"-123.613988"},"name":"Milepost 15 Access (Takeout above the Narrows)","distance":"8.28","photoID":"cAecDvzpuhBYN3X7W2Sjb","difficulty":"N/A","approximate":false}],"alerts":[{"id":"bTHZygMpVczcvTlEij0q2","title":"Park and Play spot at MP12 is GONE","post_date":"2026-03-31 23:34:50+00","detail":"This news according to James from Tillamook: a landslide from river right added large boulders that have changed the flow and ruined the playspot at the Narrows takeout. It used to be optimal at 1200-1500cfs.","user":{"name":"Teresa Gryder"}}],"photos":[{"id":"nrGPkGbAM1hsD2M0Vo6Y9","ext":"jpg","post_id":"mg3IALDEHTjgB7XE7wTYu","description":"","caption":"Footbridge.","poi_name":null,"subject":"","author":"Jacob Cruser","poi_id":null,"photo_date":"2026-01-11T15:30:17.000Z","updated_at":"2026-03-07T15:30:18.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/nrGPkGbAM1hsD2M0Vo6Y9","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/nrGPkGbAM1hsD2M0Vo6Y9"}},{"id":"TuKbmuTbpMujXu1KHl5Nd","ext":"jpg","post_id":"jcVrDnI8sFptPrYmZLCRH","description":"This shot at 1500cfs in February.","caption":"Green Wilson Water","poi_name":null,"subject":"","author":"Teresa Gryder","poi_id":null,"photo_date":"2026-02-27T22:56:49.000Z","updated_at":"2026-02-28T22:56:49.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/TuKbmuTbpMujXu1KHl5Nd","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/TuKbmuTbpMujXu1KHl5Nd"}},{"id":"J7ll2lghR6Mo7buaha1Ls","ext":"jpg","post_id":"mg3IALDEHTjgB7XE7wTYu","description":"<p>The Narrows was III+ at 2,200 cfs, fun and straight forward waves, the easiest entrance (top of photo) was center-left.</p>","caption":"The Narrows.","poi_name":null,"subject":"","author":"Jacob Cruser","poi_id":null,"photo_date":"2026-01-11T15:30:17.000Z","updated_at":"2026-03-07T15:30:18.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/J7ll2lghR6Mo7buaha1Ls","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/J7ll2lghR6Mo7buaha1Ls"}},{"id":"qrjrJa38uwM3gw1TbiJBM","ext":"jpg","post_id":"vHLcVXtPUzByabaqaEfrZ","description":"","caption":"Looking upstream at tortellini rapid","poi_name":null,"subject":"Rocky but not too low","author":"Teresa Gryder","poi_id":null,"photo_date":"2026-03-31T23:52:00.000Z","updated_at":"2026-03-31T23:52:00.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/qrjrJa38uwM3gw1TbiJBM","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/qrjrJa38uwM3gw1TbiJBM"}},{"id":"yM5Qh4Kk4bGBAt5imqXky","ext":"jpg","post_id":"mg3IALDEHTjgB7XE7wTYu","description":"<p>The Pinch at the bottom of the Narrows at 2,200 cfs. It was no factor at this flow.</p>","caption":"Pinch in the Narrows","poi_name":null,"subject":"","author":"Jacob Cruser","poi_id":null,"photo_date":"2026-01-11T15:30:17.000Z","updated_at":"2026-03-07T15:30:18.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/yM5Qh4Kk4bGBAt5imqXky","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/yM5Qh4Kk4bGBAt5imqXky"}},{"id":"fXxR5ePIvr9651N4Ng2T5","ext":"jpg","post_id":"mg3IALDEHTjgB7XE7wTYu","description":"<p>Coming through the bottom ramps at the Ledges.</p>","caption":"Bottom of the Ledges","poi_name":null,"subject":"","author":"Jacob Cruser","poi_id":null,"photo_date":"2026-01-11T15:30:17.000Z","updated_at":"2026-03-07T15:30:18.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/fXxR5ePIvr9651N4Ng2T5","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/fXxR5ePIvr9651N4Ng2T5"}},{"id":"m5hGLR6nBjy9sE78PbKzo","ext":"jpg","post_id":"vHLcVXtPUzByabaqaEfrZ","description":"<p>Map showing the Jordan Creek confluence, Cedar Butte bridge and parking area.</p>","caption":"Cedar Butte Road ACCESS","poi_name":null,"subject":"Satellite view","author":"Teresa Gryder","poi_id":"vkhNF6UPjYJ4h8RuNBPnh","photo_date":"2026-03-31T23:52:00.000Z","updated_at":"2026-03-31T23:52:00.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/m5hGLR6nBjy9sE78PbKzo","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/m5hGLR6nBjy9sE78PbKzo"}},{"id":"cAecDvzpuhBYN3X7W2Sjb","ext":"jpg","post_id":"vHLcVXtPUzByabaqaEfrZ","description":"","caption":"Canoe Club taking out at MP15","poi_name":null,"subject":"It's just a pullout along the guardrail","author":"Teresa Gryder","poi_id":"117181","photo_date":"2026-03-31T23:52:00.000Z","updated_at":"2026-03-31T23:52:00.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/cAecDvzpuhBYN3X7W2Sjb","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/cAecDvzpuhBYN3X7W2Sjb"}},{"id":"890603","ext":"jpg","post_id":"894535","description":null,"caption":"Jones Creek Bridge","poi_name":null,"subject":null,"author":"Sam Morrison","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/890603","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/890603"}},{"id":"873378","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"873377","description":"","caption":"Wilson River","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"Thomas O'Keefe","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/873378","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/873378"}},{"id":"873379","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"873377","description":"","caption":"Wilson River","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"Thomas O'Keefe","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/873379","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/873379"}},{"id":"873380","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"873377","description":"","caption":"Wilson River","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"Thomas O'Keefe","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/873380","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/873380"}},{"id":"873382","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"873377","description":"","caption":"Wilson River","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"Thomas O'Keefe","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/873382","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/873382"}},{"id":"873381","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"873377","description":"","caption":"Wilson River","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"Thomas O'Keefe","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/873381","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/873381"}}],"detail":{"id":"1569","permitUrl":"","permitInfo":"","description":"The Wilson parallels Highway 6, a major state highway between Portland and Tillamook, and much of the river is visible from the road. The rock is basalt and the channels are mostly deep, with an occasional cobble bar section where wood can hang up. The Wilson is popular with fishermen and relations between fishing and boating populations have been strained: be diplomatic and give them lots of room. The character of the river changes dramatically with different flows.\n\nAt 850-2,000cfs this section has good strong currents, nice fast ferries, eddy moves and attainments, and some excellent surfing waves and holes. It is one of the best class III skills runs in the region and would be suitable for slalom training. It does not feel low! It has giant eddies and good eddy service for lots of interesting smallish playspots. It's said that 500 cfs is runnable in kayaks, but the run is of a much lower quality. 900 is still enough for rafts.\n\nBetween 2000-3000 the Wilson develops funny water (seams, boils and whirlpools) that can mess with boaters in smaller boats who lack edge control, while remaining class III. Swims can be long in places. This is not a great level for boaters whose skills are maxed out in class III whitewater, yet a wonderful level for skilled paddlers in half slices looking for eddylines to play in. There are also a few holes to avoid and a few to play in. While still good fun it is less useful as a skill builder at these flows and more of a challenge run for the tentative class III paddler, while class IV boaters will find the class III rating is still accurate.\n\nAbove 3,000cfs it becomes quite continuous for long sections making the required skill level more like class IV (unless you're in a raft and know where you are going). At 2,000-4,000 the Wilson is a reasonable training ground for a paddler who wants to gain confidence in swirly water. The boil and seams on the Wilson are similar to what you'll encounter at the bottom of rapids on the Grand Canyon, on a smaller scale. Here is a [video of the river at 4,800 cfs](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fbUu_eOnFRo) (the Narrows starts at 5:00).\n\nRafters tend to enjoy this run at higher flows than kayakers because they are less affected by the funny water. Hollywood develops the biggest wave train on the river at around 6,000 cfs, plenty big enough to flip 16 footers end for end.\n\nThe put-in is at Highway 6 milepost 22.6 at the Jones Creek Day Use Area. Turn off the highway and cross the bridge. The day use area is on the downstream river right side of the bridge. The photo of the put-in shown is of people launching on river left above the bridge, which is also possible but less civilized.\n\nThere are several possible takeouts making it possible to do shorter play runs or a longer float. From 1100-2000cfs the run from Jones Creek to the Footbridge is a great 3 mile play/skills run finishing with a scenic class III+ gorge (check for wood from a highway pullout while running shuttle). From 1400 up excellent play continues to Jordan creek making for a 4 mile run, with possible access on river left immediately below Jordan's confluence, or river right at the next bridge. There is more excellent play downstream from Jordan which is best developed at 3,000-5,000cfs and suitable for class IV paddlers in playboats. Continuing the whole 8 miles to milepost 15 means you won't have time to work every feature, but at lower flows it's is a nice class III run for folks who don't play as much.","averageGradient":"35","maxGradient":null,"length":"8.3","correlations":[{"reachID":"1569","isPrimary":true,"correlationDetails":{"metric":"cfs","belowRecommendedRangeComment":"Hardshells only at the bottom end of range.  Rafts OK down to 900cfs.","lowRunnableRangeComment":"1100cfs turns out to be a \"dud\" flow; the play is better at 850cfs.","mediumRunnableRangeComment":null,"highRunnableRangeComment":null,"aboveRecommendedRangeComment":null,"lowRunnableAdjustedDifficulty":null,"highRunnableAdjustedDifficulty":null,"beginLowRunnable":"600","beginMediumRunnable":"1200","beginHighRunnable":"2400","endHighRunnable":"7000"},"gaugeInfo":{"name":"WILSON RIVER NEAR TILLAMOOK, OR","gaugeSource":"USGS","gaugeSourceIdentifier":"14301500","gaugeFetchStatus":"ACTIVE","latestFlowReading":{"value":"250","dateTime":"2026-05-12T02:30:00.000Z","qualifier":"NORMAL"},"latestStageReading":{"value":"